<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Messina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Messina is a port city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the strait that separates the island from the mainland. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in the 8th century BCE as Zancle and renamed Messana, it was seized by the Mamertines, whose appeal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> in 264 BCE. Taken by the Normans in 1061, it rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1674 and was largely destroyed by an earthquake in 1908.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/messina.jpg" alt="Messina" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Hoernigk · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messina_2003.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Palermo fell in 831 and became the base of Muslim rule; <b>Messina</b> followed in 842 or 843, Syracuse in 878 and Taormina in 902, though pockets of Christian resistance held out until 967.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The island was fertile and exported grain, while Palermo, <b>Messina</b> and the Apulian ports traded with North Africa, the Levant and the Italian maritime cities; silk working, introduced in Byzantine and Islamic times, was reinforced when Greek silk workers were carried off from Thebes and Corinth in 1147.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It put down the Calabrian revolution of 1847, the Sicilian independence revolution of 1848 and, with hired Swiss Guards, the Neapolitan revolution of May 1848, retaking the mainland by July, <b>Messina</b> by September and Palermo on 15 May 1849.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A 14th-century manuscript now in <b>Messina</b> pairs it with acclamations for King Frederick II of Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cherubikon">Cherubikon (the Cherubic Hymn)</a></li><li><blockquote>A powerful earthquake and tsunami struck the Strait of <b>Messina</b>, destroying Messina in Sicily and Reggio Calabria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-28">28 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles fled across the Straits of <b>Messina</b> to his Kingdom of Naples.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-4">4 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
